## Bootstrapping (Proportion of 2+ Replies vs Sentiment)

```{r}
# Here, we are calculating the Bootstrap Proportion of 2+ Replies for each Sentiment. The function below is used to return the mean of high-engagement metrics (this time for Replies)
prop_2plus_rep_func <- function(data, indices) {
  return(mean(data[indices] >= 2))
}

# I figured out how to make the bootstrapping process more efficient this time around compared to the Likes data without fundamentally altering the results. Here, we comprehensively run the bootstrap for each Sentiment, and once again set the seed for reproducibility
sentiments <- c("Positive", "Neutral", "Negative")
for (s in sentiments) {
  subset_data <- yt_data_prop_rep$Replies[yt_data_prop_rep$Sentiment == s]
  set.seed(123)
  boot_res <- boot(data = subset_data, statistic = prop_2plus_rep_func, R = 1000)
  
# Now, we calculate the 95% confidence intervals (CIs) and actual value for all Sentiments. The results used for Figure F in the final study were derived from these values
  ci <- boot.ci(boot_res, type = "perc")
  boot_df <- data.frame(props = boot_res$t)
  
# Finally, we create a histogram that visualizes the bootstrapped results for conveinence
  p <- ggplot(boot_df, aes(x = props)) +
    geom_histogram(bins = 30, fill = "#9b59b6", color = "white", alpha = 0.8) +
    geom_vline(xintercept = ci$percent[4], color = "red", linetype = "dashed") +
    geom_vline(xintercept = ci$percent[5], color = "red", linetype = "dashed") +
    geom_vline(xintercept = boot_res$t0, color = "black", size = 1.2) +
    scale_x_continuous(labels = percent_format(accuracy = 0.1)) +
    labs(title = paste("Bootstrap Distribution: % of", s, "Comments with 2+ Replies"),
         subtitle = paste("Actual:", round(boot_res$t0*100, 2), "%"),
         x = "Proportion", y = "Frequency") +
    theme_minimal()
  print(p)
}
```

